<p><strong>Staff scheduling software for a salon or spa in Nepal records when each stylist or therapist works and at which branch, makes them bookable only while they are on shift, handles transfers between branches and carries attendance into payroll. It replaces the WhatsApp roster and the shared spreadsheet.</strong></p>
<p>With one branch, a whiteboard roster can work. With two or more, staff cover each other, shifts change at short notice and the person taking bookings does not always know who is in. That is where bookings go wrong.</p>
<h2>Schedules that drive booking</h2>
<p>If the schedule and the booking calendar are separate, clients get booked with someone who is off. When the schedule is the source of truth, only working staff can be booked. See <a href="/features/staff-management/">staff management</a>.</p>
<h2>Transfers between branches</h2>
<p>A therapist covering another branch for a week should be bookable there, and their work should still count toward their pay. Look for software that supports transfers rather than treating each branch as a separate list.</p>
<h2>Attendance into payroll</h2>
<p>Late arrivals, absences and commission affect pay. When attendance and bookings live together, <a href="/features/payroll/">payroll</a> starts from real data. For the commission side, read <a href="/blog/therapist-commission-payroll-nepal/">how to calculate therapist commission and payroll in Nepal</a>.</p>
<h2>Who can see and change what</h2>
<p>Owners, managers and front desk need different access. See <a href="/features/role-based-access/">role-based access</a>.</p>
